 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Encoding involves block-wise operations (averaging, subtraction, thresholding, vector quantization). The complexity is linear with respect to the number of voxels, $O(N_x N_y N_z)$, assuming constant-time codebook lookup [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The compressed representation size is $3 \times (N_x/4)(N_y/4)(N_z/4)$ bytes for the index texture, plus the size of the codebooks ($256 \times 64 \times \text{bytes} + 256 \times 8 \times \text{bytes}$) and address texture [S1, unofficial]. This results in a significant compression ratio compared to the original $N_x N_y N_z$ voxels.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources. [S1, unofficial]
-*   **Scaling:** The method scales well with volume size as it is block-based. The GPU rendering aspect allows for efficient parallel reconstruction [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High compression ratios due to vector quantization and thresholding; efficient hardware decoding via texture lookups; enhanced fidelity by assembling signal from multiple bands [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Lossy compression; requires pre-computed codebooks; block artifacts may be visible if thresholding is too aggressive; limited to scalar fields [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Suitable for compression domain volume rendering where memory bandwidth is a bottleneck and high compression ratios are needed [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When lossless compression is required or when the data does not exhibit the spatial correlation assumed by the block-based decomposition [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Encoding involves partitioning, averaging, and vector quantization. Vector quantization requires comparing each difference vector against all 256 codewords, which is $O(N \cdot L)$ where $N$ is the number of blocks and $L$ is the codebook size (256). Decoding is efficient, relying on texture lookups [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The compressed representation is significantly smaller than the original volume. The index texture is $1/4$ the size of the original volume in each dimension (total $1/64$ the voxels), with 3 bytes per texel. Codebooks are small fixed-size textures ($256 \times 64$ and $256 \times 8$) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** The method was implemented on an ATI 9700 GPU, exploiting per-fragment arithmetic and dependent texture fetches [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Maximizes codeword usage for significant vectors via thresholding; enhances fidelity by assembling signals from separate bands; hardware-friendly decoding via texture lookups [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Lossy compression; requires pre-computed codebooks; block artifacts may occur at block boundaries; mean value quantization error [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Suitable for compression domain volume rendering on hardware with efficient texture lookup capabilities [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When lossless compression is required or when the volume dimensions are not divisible by 4 without padding [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Triadic frequency band decomposition, also known as hierarchical quantization, was introduced in the context of compression domain volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. It exploits the capabilities of modern GPUs (specifically ATI 9700 at the time of publication) to perform arithmetic operations and dependent texture fetches on a per-fragment basis [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to enable efficient rendering of large volumetric datasets by reducing memory footprint and bandwidth requirements [S1, unofficial].
+Triadic frequency band decomposition, also known as hierarchical quantization scheme, was introduced in the paper "Compression Domain Volume Rendering" [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to enable efficient volume rendering by compressing data in a way that is compatible with graphics hardware, specifically leveraging the ATI 9700 GPU's capabilities for per-fragment operations and dependent texture fetches [S1, unofficial]. The method partitions data into triadic frequency bands to exploit spatial correlation and allows for efficient decoding through texture-based codebook lookups [S1, unofficial].
 
